        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Driveway Sealing Mistakes That Waste Time and Money
          </h2>

          <p>
            Even with the best product in hand, application errors can undermine your results entirely. After reviewing hundreds of customer complaints and consulting with professional pavers, these are the most frequent mistakes homeowners make when sealing their driveways.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Degreasing Step</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Oil stains are the number one cause of sealer failure. When sealer is applied over an oil-contaminated area, it cannot bond to the asphalt surface. Within weeks, the sealer lifts and peels away, leaving an unsightly patch that looks worse than the original stain. Always treat oil spots with a dedicated asphalt degreaser and scrub with a stiff wire brush before washing the entire surface. For particularly stubborn stains that have penetrated deep into the asphalt, apply an oil spot primer and allow it to cure for twenty-four hours before sealing over it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying Sealer Too Thick</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                More is not better when it comes to driveway sealer. A thick coat takes significantly longer to cure, remains tacky for days, and is far more prone to cracking and peeling during the first freeze-thaw cycle. The product cannot off-gas properly when applied in heavy layers, trapping moisture and solvents underneath the surface film. Two thin coats applied in perpendicular directions always outperform a single heavy coat in both appearance and longevity. Each coat should be thin enough that you can still see the texture of the asphalt through the wet sealer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing in the Wrong Weather Conditions</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Applying sealer when rain is forecast within forty-eight hours is one of the most expensive mistakes a homeowner can make. Uncured sealer washes away completely, requiring you to start the entire process over with fresh product. Equally problematic is sealing in extreme heat above ninety degrees, which causes the product to skin over before it can properly bond, or sealing when overnight temperatures will drop below fifty degrees, which prevents the chemical curing process from completing. Always check an extended weather forecast and plan your sealing project around a window of consistently warm and dry conditions.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Neglecting Edge Work and Perimeter Preparation</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The edges of your driveway where asphalt meets grass, landscaping, or concrete walkways are the most vulnerable areas for water intrusion and cracking. Many homeowners rush through the edges with a squeegee and end up with uneven coverage, sealer on their sidewalks, or grass clippings embedded in the wet product. Take the time to edge these areas carefully with a disposable paint brush before applying the main coat with a squeegee. This creates a clean, professional border and ensures full coverage in the transition zones where water damage typically begins.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Can I seal my driveway myself or should I hire a professional?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Driveway sealing is one of the most DIY-friendly home maintenance tasks. A standard two-car driveway takes 2-4 hours and costs $100-150 in materials versus $300-600 for professional application. The key is proper surface preparation — cleaning and crack filling before sealing.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
